      "name": null,
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227445/assets/48d9d8513f45b87f9deffeffcef31e49a6dcaba0b033c91a918c1d72d87eb215.png)\n\nIn the quadrilateral $ABCD$ above, determine the measure of $∠C.$",
      "worked_solution": "A quadrilateral has $n=4$ sides. Therefore, the sum of the interior angles of a quadrilateral  must be \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{180}^{∘}\\cdot (n-2) & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot (4-2) \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding up the internal angles and solving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠A+m∠B+m∠C+m∠D & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ x+{90}^{∘}+3x+6x & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 10x+{90}^{∘} & ={360}^{∘} \\\\ 10x & ={270}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={27}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠C & =3x \\\\  & =3({27}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={81}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
